        "properties": [
            "Unnecessary sorting and intermediate work ↓",
            "Expression of ordered queries simplified"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Se": "Declare the order assumed by expressions and distinguish order-preserving from order-cavalier operators.",
            "Ep": "Apply order-aware equivalences that move, split, or embed sorts while preserving the query’s required semantics.",
            "Wv": "Push selective work before sorting and use sort-edge/edgeby to avoid sorting or aggregating rows that cannot affect the answer."
        },
        "composition": "Explicit order semantics reveal legal plan transformations; these move filters and edge selections earlier, reducing the data sorted, grouped, or joined. The benefit depends on query structure and data distribution, and requires operators to respect the declared order properties.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://vldb.org/conf/2003/papers/S11P03.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Ordering contract",
                "text": "Arrables expose ordered arrays. ASSUMING ORDER specifies what query expressions may assume, while the optimizer decides where sorting is needed and when an order-cavalier operator is legal.",
                "reference": "§§2.1–2.3",
                "page": 3
            },
            {
                "label": "Work avoided",
                "text": "An implicit first/last selection can be separated from projection and combined with sorting as sort-edge. Stable top/bottom selection avoids sorting an entire input merely to retain a small edge.",
                "reference": "§3.1, Figure 3",
                "page": 6
            },
            {
                "label": "Measured query results",
                "text": "The historical implementation is 8–21× faster for the best-profit query and 2–3× for network management than the tested commercial SQL:1999 optimizer. The first comparison varies 200–1,000 securities with 1,000 trades each; it is not a claim about current SQL engines.",
                "reference": "§4.1, Figure 7",
                "page": 9
            },
            {
                "label": "Costs and conditions",
                "text": "Transformations need order-dependency and cardinality rules. Sort splitting can add overhead when few groups exist, and edge selection saves little if it discards few rows; the paper stresses cost-based selection of rewrites.",
                "reference": "§4.1, Figures 8–9",
                "page": 10
            }
        ]
