        "properties": [
            "Repeated aggregation work ↓ when decomposable",
            "Subtotal semantics made explicit"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Gr": "Generalize GROUP BY, cross-tabs, and subtotals through one multidimensional CUBE operator.",
            "Se": "Distinguish rolled-up dimensions from ordinary values and null data through explicit aggregate markers and GROUPING information.",
            "Rw": "Derive coarser aggregates from finer aggregates when the function supports it, carrying sufficient intermediate state such as sum and count for averages."
        },
        "composition": "CUBE exposes the family of grouping combinations in one operator, allowing the implementation to share work across levels. Explicit grouping semantics preserve the meaning of subtotals. Reuse depends on the aggregate: averaging averages is insufficient without counts, and holistic functions need more than fixed-size partial summaries.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/tr-95-22.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Unified operation",
                "text": "A cube over N dimensions includes all 2^N grouping combinations. The paper illustrates that a six-dimensional cross-tab otherwise requires a 64-way union of GROUP BY expressions. This describes query structure, not a measured 64× speedup.",
                "reference": "§§2–3"
            },
            {
                "label": "Subtotal meaning",
                "text": "The proposal describes an ALL marker for aggregated dimensions and GROUPING tests to identify it. It also discusses using NULL plus grouping indicators as an implementation alternative; an ordinary data NULL must remain distinguishable from a rolled-up dimension.",
                "reference": "§3",
                "page": 8
            },
            {
                "label": "When reuse is valid",
                "text": "Distributive aggregates such as SUM combine partial results directly. Algebraic aggregates require bounded state: AVG carries both sum and count. The described approach does not offer the same compact-summary optimization for holistic aggregates such as median.",
                "reference": "§5",
                "page": 10
            },
            {
                "label": "Output and memory costs",
                "text": "Sharing aggregation work does not remove the exponential number of grouping combinations. Large cubes may require partitioning or sorting instead of in-memory arrays, and sparse representations can avoid storing absent combinations. The paper presents semantics and implementation strategies rather than an end-to-end speedup benchmark.",
                "reference": "§§5–6",
                "page": 10
            }
        ],
