        "system": "R* presumed-outcome protocols",
        "mechanism": "Recovery defaults + outcome-specific logging + read-only votes",
        "properties": [
            "Selected log forces and acknowledgements ↓",
            "Failure-time blocking remains"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Cc": "Choose a presumed outcome and specialize commit processing for the expected transaction mix; detect read-only subtrees during voting.",
            "Ig": "Make forgotten state imply a safe recovery outcome, with mandatory durable information where that inference would otherwise be ambiguous.",
            "Wv": "Omit acknowledgements, forced log writes, or second-phase messages only when the recovery rules and read-only status make them unnecessary."
        },
        "composition": "Presumed Abort and Presumed Commit reduce different parts of two-phase commit by changing what missing recovery information means. Read-only votes remove participants that have no updates to resolve. The variants preserve atomic commit but have different normal-path costs and retain blocking after coordinator failure.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~yxy/cs764-f20/papers/R-XactMgmt.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Presumed Abort",
                "text": "A coordinator can forget an abort because later inquiries without a retained decision are answered ABORT. Abort acknowledgements and forced abort records can therefore be removed. Read-only participants vote READ, release locks, and need no second-phase decision message.",
                "reference": "§3",
                "page": 8
            },
            {
                "label": "Presumed Commit is not a simple reversal",
                "text": "Before sending PREPARE, a coordinator must force a collecting record containing participant identities. Otherwise a crash before the decision could make an undecided transaction look committed. Commit acknowledgements are removed, while abort handling must retain sufficient evidence.",
                "reference": "§4",
                "page": 11
            },
            {
                "label": "Costs depend on transaction shape",
                "text": "For the two-level committing transactions analyzed in §5, PC saves n acknowledgements and n−1 forced log writes versus PA when n>1 subordinates update. Completely read-only PA transactions require no log records or second-phase messages, but still exchange PREPARE and READ votes.",
                "reference": "§§3, 5",
                "page": 13
            },
            {
                "label": "Failure limit",
                "text": "Both protocols remain blocking: a prepared participant may wait to reconnect with its coordinator to learn the outcome. The paper discusses extensions to reduce blocking but states that those extensions were not implemented.",
                "reference": "§5",
                "page": 14
            }
        ],
