        "composition": "DAG-Explainer first mines a finite set of frequent subgraphs, then scores explanation sets by model recognition, support, cross-class denial, and size. Randomized greedy selection makes optimization over that set cheap, accepting approximate optimality. Its expected approximation guarantee can weaken sharply as the candidate set grows; it does not establish near-optimal explanations over all possible graphs. The evaluation also uses repeated runs and majority voting for final output, distinct from the single-run theoretical guarantee. Candidate mining can dominate total cost, but can be reused across models on the same dataset.",
