        "principleNotes": {
            "Cp": "Encode whether each tuple outranks the target with indicator constraints and solve for valid nonnegative ranking weights under user-supplied restrictions.",
            "Ep": "For a fixed competitor and rectangular weight region, replace checking every weight vector with an equivalent check at that competitor’s worst-case corner.",
            "Qr": "Use a sufficient monotonic-core condition and optional conservative clustering to make region search tractable, accepting smaller regions or worse reported ranks while preserving returned-solution validity."
        },
        "composition": "The exact encoding answers whether weights can admit a tuple to top-k, returns a witness weight vector, and finds its best attainable rank via binary search over k. For BOX, per-competitor corner checks eliminate continuous enumeration, but requiring a fixed set of competitors to lose throughout the box is only sufficient, so the largest valid box may be missed. Optional cluster representatives further reduce indicator count conservatively. Feasibility guarantees concern the stated nonnegative linear scoring model and weight constraints; they do not certify that a ranking policy is fair in a broader sense.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol16/p2377-chen.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Exact ranking questions",
                "text": "Indicator variables encode rank. SAT gives feasibility, its satisfying assignment answers POINT, and binary search over k answers BEST. The ranking model uses nonnegative linear weights with additional permitted constraints.",
                "reference": "§2; §3.1–3.2"
            },
            {
                "label": "Sound inner boxes",
                "text": "Lemma 9 reduces each competitor’s linear score difference over a box to its worst corner. Lemma 10 and Theorem 11 supply a sufficient monotonic-core condition, which may yield a smaller box than the true optimum.",
                "reference": "§3.3; Lemmas 9–10; Theorem 11"
            },
            {
                "label": "Measured search",
                "text": "On the selected NBA player-season queries, BEST takes about six seconds on average. Sampling finds the correct best rank in 40% of cases at six seconds and 60% at sixty seconds, without certifying that no better rank exists.",
                "reference": "§6.3; Figure 5"
            },
            {
                "label": "Conservatism and cost",
                "text": "Cluster maxima overcount possible competitors, preserving valid returned weights but potentially losing solutions. In one Triangle BOX case, perimeter optimization takes about three seconds while volume optimization takes over an hour; these optimize different objectives.",
                "reference": "§5.3; §6.6; Figure 8"
            }
        ],
