        "system": "kPlexS",
        "mechanism": "prune to bounded neighborhoods and reuse pairwise search bounds",
        "properties": [
            "Search space ↓",
            "Repeated bound computation ↓",
            "Local quadratic storage ↑",
            "Large-solution optimum preserved"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Ig": "Use degree, common-neighbor, and two-hop containment properties that every qualifying k-plex must satisfy to justify graph reduction and local subproblem coverage.",
            "Wv": "Prune vertices, edges, and search branches that cannot beat the incumbent within the stated solution-size regime.",
            "Rw": "Maintain degrees and common-neighbor counts incrementally during branching and undo, checking only bounds affected by each update.",
            "Rs": "Spend quadratic memory on adjacency and pairwise-count matrices for small extracted subgraphs to enable cheap lookups and stronger second-order pruning."
        },
        "composition": "A k-plex of size at least 2k−1 lies within two hops of any of its vertices. kPlexS uses that invariant to search smaller extracted subgraphs, applying core/truss pruning as the incumbent improves. BBMatrix then uses adjacency and common-neighbor matrices for stronger reduction and bounding, reusing updated counts across recursive search. These steps preserve the optimum when a qualifying large k-plex exists; the paper explicitly permits an arbitrary k-plex when none reaches 2k−1. A returned size of 2k−2 is also certified maximum, but smaller returns may require another solver for the unrestricted optimum. The NP-hard search remains exponential in difficult cases, and quadratic local storage limits subproblem size.",
