        "composition": "MITra expresses traversal order through ranks, frontier width, and frontier organization, while an edge function describes application computation. The engine aligns source instances, reads shared edges once, and invokes masked vector operators over their separate states. For reachability, vertex-ID ranking can share work across BFS depth levels without changing the answer. That rewrite is query-specific: rank choices and optional tracking-free execution are not universally interchangeable. The reachability theorem bounds edge accesses, not elapsed time. Source-dependent arrays and frontier masks consume memory, and small source sets on sparse graphs may offer too little reuse to recover framework overhead.",
