        "system": "Feedback-driven entity-query mediator (Hybrid)",
        "mechanism": "share term-reward learning and specialize when feedback shows poor fit",
        "properties": [
            "Early query effectiveness ↑",
            "Feedback reused across entities",
            "Manual query formulation ↓",
            "Exploration and model-state cost ↑"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "La": "Approximate a term’s expected retrieval reward from lexical, distributional, and entity-context features using a learned linear model.",
            "Ad": "Update the policy from relevance feedback and instantiate an entity-specific model when shared performance plateaus and that entity’s recent reward is poor.",
            "Rw": "Transfer feedback through a shared model across entities, then warm-start a specialized model with that entity’s past feedback and remove its contribution from shared statistics.",
            "Pd": "Use contextual-bandit confidence bounds to explore uncertain candidate terms while exploiting terms predicted to retrieve relevant answers."
        },
        "composition": "The mediator formulates a bounded-length keyword query by selecting candidate terms from the entity, optionally expanded with external-result terms. LinUCB shares a feature-based reward model across entities to learn useful queries with fewer interactions. Hybrid monitors recent rewards and moves poorly served entities to specialized models, initialized from their history. The term-by-term policy assumes sufficiently independent term contributions and a useful feature representation; it is not a guarantee of the globally best keyword combination. Exploration can temporarily worsen results, real feedback may be costly or noisy, and specialization increases retained state. The evaluated feedback is simulated from ground truth with a static BM25 backend, and merging the retrieved data is outside the system’s scope.",
