        "system": "Seeded Cache and Reconstruct",
        "mechanism": "share a noisy basis and answers while preserving each analyst’s budget entitlement",
        "properties": [
            "Cross-analyst answer reuse ↑",
            "Expected participation guarantee",
            "Noisy reconstruction after exhaustion",
            "Fairness limits total utility"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Fs": "Define sharing incentive relative to independent execution and analyst monotonicity relative to joining collectives, using expected numbers of sufficiently accurate answers.",
            "Is": "Keep an analyst’s remaining privacy entitlement separately accounted after the shared seed, preventing other analysts from consuming that reserved budget.",
            "Rw": "Pool a privately measured basis and cached query answers; reconstruct further linear queries from that shared information after an analyst’s own budget is exhausted."
        },
        "composition": "The equation describes the paper’s Seeded Cache and Reconstruct mechanism. Analysts contribute a fixed fraction of their budget to a shared basis, then use their reserved remainder for uncached queries while reusing cached answers across analysts. Once an analyst’s budget runs out, the system reconstructs answers from the cache. Direct answers deliberately take precedence over reconstruction while budget remains, preserving the monotonicity argument. The guarantee concerns expected utility relative to the same independent mechanism, not exact answers or every realized run. The paper also offers an alternative Query Scheduler that reorders access to an existing mechanism: it trades waiting time for conditional sharing-incentive guarantees and does not inherit SCR’s analyst-monotonicity guarantee. These alternatives are not sequential stages of one system.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol16/p816-pujol.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Fairness contract",
                "text": "Sharing incentive compares expected utility with an analyst’s independent execution; analyst monotonicity requires that adding a participant and their budget does not lower another participant’s expected utility.",
                "reference": "§3.2–3.3"
            },
            {
                "label": "SCR mechanism and restriction",
                "text": "A shared full-rank seed enables reconstruction of any linear query, but not necessarily within the error threshold. Queries use direct budget before reconstruction; removing this restriction can break the fairness argument.",
                "reference": "§6; Lemma 3; §6.1"
            },
            {
                "label": "Distinct scheduling alternative",
                "text": "Round-robin and randomized schedulers restrict analyst arrival order. Their sharing-incentive results require suitable scaling of the underlying mechanism, and scheduled execution can stall when a chosen analyst has no query ready.",
                "reference": "§7.1–7.3; Theorems 7–9"
            },
            {
                "label": "Measured fairness and delay",
                "text": "With ten analysts, ε=1 and error threshold 0.01, unmodified shared PMW can give some analysts roughly three times fewer useful answers than independent execution. Scheduling can take up to twice as many simulated time steps; SCR trades some aggregate utility for stronger fairness.",
                "reference": "§8.1; §8.4; Figure 3"
            }
        ],
        "cardUpdates": {
            "primary_design_archetype": "shared private-answer reuse with isolated analyst entitlements and formal fairness",
            "limiting_design_assumption": "Analysts must choose between independent privacy budgets without reuse and a shared pool whose arrival order can disadvantage some participants.",
            "one_sentence_mechanism": "Seeded Cache and Reconstruct shares a noisy basis and cached answers while reserving per-analyst privacy budgets, then reconstructs further answers after exhaustion to preserve expected participation incentives.",
            "concrete_numbers": [
                {
                    "number_or_delta": "10 analysts; ε=1; error threshold 0.01",
                    "meaning": "Reported multi-analyst experiment, with equal budget shares and varied arrival skew.",
                    "pointer": "§8.1; §8.4"
                },
                {
                    "number_or_delta": "Up to 3× more useful answers independently",
                    "meaning": "Observed disadvantage of unmodified pooled PMW for some analysts; motivates the fairness contract rather than measuring SCR speedup.",
                    "pointer": "§8.4.2"
                },
                {
                    "number_or_delta": "Up to 2× simulated completion steps",
                    "meaning": "Cost of the alternative Query Schedulers under skewed arrivals; not a runtime penalty of SCR.",
                    "pointer": "§8.4.4"
                }
            ],
            "physical_properties": [
                {
                    "property": "cross-analyst reuse",
                    "direction": "up",
                    "concrete_number": "Shared seed and noisy-answer cache",
                    "pointer": "§6"
                },
                {
                    "property": "participation fairness",
                    "direction": "expected guarantee",
                    "concrete_number": "SCR is analyst monotonic relative to independent execution",
                    "pointer": "§3.2; Lemma 3"
                },
                {
                    "property": "post-budget answer quality",
                    "direction": "conditional",
                    "concrete_number": "Reconstruction is possible but may exceed the error threshold",
                    "pointer": "§6.1"
                },
                {
                    "property": "aggregate utility or waiting",
                    "direction": "tradeoff",
                    "concrete_number": "SCR constrains reuse policy; alternative schedulers can stall",
                    "pointer": "§6.1; §7.3; §8.4"
                }
            ],
            "key_deltas": [
                {
                    "delta_id": "Δ1",
                    "summary": "Pool a privately measured seed and shared answers while maintaining each analyst’s remaining budget entitlement.",
                    "primary_metric_improved": "reuse without sacrificing expected participation incentives",
                    "tradeoffs_or_costs": "Seed choice affects utility, and analysts with unrelated workloads may gain little from sharing."
                },
                {
                    "delta_id": "Δ2",
                    "summary": "Use direct measurements while budget remains, then reconstruct further queries from the shared cache.",
                    "primary_metric_improved": "continued query coverage after individual budget exhaustion",
                    "tradeoffs_or_costs": "Reconstructed answers may be inaccurate; the fairness-preserving policy can answer fewer useful queries overall than an unconstrained shared mechanism."
                }
            ]
        }
