        "composition": "ATP first randomizes a trajectory’s anchor and radius to narrow the region in which points will be sampled. Within two complementary copies, alternate points act as private pivots and randomized directions from neighboring pivots restrict each remaining point’s candidate set. The copies are then combined using their perturbed outputs. This preserves useful geometry better than independent whole-domain perturbation in many tests, but consumes budget on region and direction estimation before sampling points. Noisy constraints can exclude useful locations, and small privacy budgets favor coarse directions. The method assumes a public finite location universe and retains the input point count; the experiments exclude temporal information. Its stronger geometric restriction can worsen hotspot counts relative to TP without the anchor stage.",
