        "system": "gCore with KP-tree indexing",
        "mechanism": "generalize cross-layer cohesion and reuse nested cores in a compact parameter index",
        "properties": [
            "Cross-layer expressiveness ↑",
            "Repeated query work ↓",
            "Exact indexed retrieval",
            "Index construction and storage ↑"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Gr": "Extend pillar-graph core models to layers with different vertex sets and arbitrary cross-layer edges, adding fractional coverage of neighbors in cohesive auxiliary layers.",
            "Ig": "Use uniqueness and containment under stronger degree or coverage thresholds to organize the parameter space and prove safe pruning and compaction.",
            "Rw": "Compute a stricter child core from its parent’s surviving subgraph, then materialize nested results so later queries reuse the decomposition.",
            "Wv": "Prune descendants of empty cores and eliminate or merge redundant parameter subtrees while retaining exact retrievability."
        },
        "composition": "A gCore is a maximal set on one selected layer whose vertices satisfy an intra-layer degree threshold and retain prescribed fractions of their cross-layer neighbors in cohesive cores on the other layers. The model supports different vertex sets across layers; it does not merely require the same community everywhere. Stronger thresholds produce nested results. The KP-tree uses that containment to reuse parent computations, skip empty regions, and store differences rather than repeated full sets. Compaction removes equivalent index structure without relaxing the answer. Fast retrieval pays for preprocessing and storage: full parameter enumeration is infeasible for several large datasets, and dynamic index maintenance is left to future work.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol16/p3201-zou.pdf",
        "evidence": [
            {
                "label": "Generalized cohesion contract",
                "text": "Definition 3.3 combines a core on the selected layer with nonempty auxiliary-layer cores covering required fractions of each selected vertex’s cross-layer neighbors. Pillar models are special cases.",
                "reference": "§3.1–3.2"
            },
            {
                "label": "Exact reuse and pruning",
                "text": "Containment permits computing stronger cores on parent subgraphs and pruning empty descendants. KP-trees materialize results with difference sets; compaction preserves retrieval.",
                "reference": "§5.2–7"
            },
            {
                "label": "Query and storage gains",
                "text": "Indexed GCS+ is 10–10,000× faster than GCS on most datasets, but only 2.4–2.9× on 9-NG. Combined compaction reduces index storage by 41–98%.",
                "reference": "§8.3.1–8.3.2"
            },
            {
                "label": "Preprocessing scope",
                "text": "Complete indexes are infeasible for Movie and two Aminer variants, so construction experiments use 1,000 sampled degree vectors. Some configurations exceed 12 hours or memory; dynamic maintenance is future work.",
                "reference": "§8.3.2; §9"
            }
        ],
