        "principleNotes": {
            "Cp": "Use binary linear optimization to choose valid chunk/segment configurations jointly, subject to storage budgets and sorting/index compatibility constraints.",
            "Cm": "Populate the objective with measured scan costs, workload frequencies, selectivity effects, and storage penalties rather than fixed tuning rules.",
            "Rs": "Trade DRAM capacity against SSD access and compression/decompression work, spending the memory budget where the modeled workload benefits most."
        },
        "composition": "The optimizer jointly chooses sorting, encoding, indexes, and DRAM/SSD placement instead of fixing one dimension before tuning the others. CCD models enumerated chunk-wide dependencies; SMS retains sorting interactions with segment-level costs; ISE uses a cheaper independence approximation. These are alternative models, not successive stages. Binary decisions enforce budgets and valid configurations, while benchmark-derived costs predict workload performance. Solver optimality applies to the chosen options and cost model, not automatically to real runtime. Extensions can penalize reconfiguration or protect against specified workload scenarios. The evaluated scan-oriented workload has few columns; larger option spaces, inaccurate cost estimates, and configuration changes add substantial cost.",
