        "system": "Simulation covering index for positive XML queries",
        "mechanism": "merge nodes indistinguishable by positive branching-path queries and recover answers through their extents",
        "properties": [
            "Quotient index nodes ≤ baseline",
            "Supported-query answers exact",
            "Negation and order-sensitive indexing excluded",
            "Preprocessing and extent storage remain"
        ],
        "principleNotes": {
            "Se": "Make the supported query class explicit: the nontrivial covering-index guarantee applies to absolute positive branching-path queries, without negation or sibling-order axes.",
            "Ig": "Merge mutually forward-and-backward-simulating nodes because no supported query can distinguish members of an equivalence class.",
            "Rw": "Precompute the quotient and its original-node extents once for use by subsequent supported queries.",
            "Wv": "Evaluate supported queries on the smaller quotient and expand selected extents without traversing the original document graph."
        },
        "composition": "Negation-free branching-path queries cannot distinguish some nodes that bisimulation keeps separate. The paper proves that mutual forward/backward simulation gives the coarsest node partition preserving every absolute BPQ+ query, so the quotient is a smallest covering index in this partition-based model. Query results remain exact: selected quotient nodes expand to their original-node extents. This reduces supported expressiveness rather than answer quality. The benefit is a theorem and a constructed exponential separation, not an empirical compression ratio. Extent storage and simulation construction remain costs; relative queries or sibling-order-sensitive Core XPath can distinguish all original nodes and require an uncompressed covering partition.",
