        "composition": "Continuous Windowing maintains the moving objects within distance d of the query point, retaining that set in memory. Boundary events bring objects into or out of the candidate set; neighbor-order events update the k nearest answers within it. As long as the set contains at least k objects and fits the configured memory limit, farther objects cannot beat the candidate answers without first crossing the tracked boundary. Violating either size constraint requires restarting with a new radius; changing the query trajectory also invalidates scheduled events. This exchanges global-index maintenance and searches for additional event processing and an event queue. The experiments use a fixed radius and a moving query whose trajectory is not updated; automatic radius selection is future work. Benefits reverse sharply without object updates.",
