        "composition": "Providers form privacy groups and pass a randomized bit vector around a ring. The index host receives group summaries rather than individual content vectors and maps a keyword query to every member of matching groups. Searchers then contact those providers, which enforce their own access policies. The ideal PPI definition never excludes matching providers and requires sufficient ambiguity, but the finite-round construction only approaches its conditions probabilistically; group summaries can omit true bits, and some queries may not have enough false positives. The paper’s innocence claims are specific to its index/adversary model, not differential privacy or an arbitrary-side-information guarantee. A malicious successor can infer predecessor content with the paper’s stated probability up to 0.71, and colluding neighbors can expose contributions. Query privacy and provider anonymity are outside scope.",
